facts:
At a zero of **multiplicity 1**, the curve **cuts** straight through the $x$-axis.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-2.2,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.8) -- (0,2.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.2:3.2,smooth,variable=\x,samples=60] plot ({\x},{0.9*(\x-1)+0.05*(\x-1)*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\filldraw (1,0) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

At a zero of **even multiplicity** ($m = 2, 4, \ldots$), the curve **touches** the $x$-axis and turns back without crossing.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-2.2,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-0.9) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.2:3.2,smooth,variable=\x,samples=60] plot ({\x},{0.55*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\filldraw (1,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

At a zero of **odd multiplicity $\ge 3$** ($m = 3, 5, \ldots$), the curve **flattens** along the $x$-axis as it crosses.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[<->] (-2.2,0) -- (3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->] (0,-2.7) -- (0,2.7) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.2:3.2,smooth,variable=\x,samples=60] plot ({\x},{0.22*(\x-1)*(\x-1)*(\x-1)});
\filldraw (1,0) circle (2pt) node[below] {$\alpha$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
The sketch shows a polynomial of degree $4$. Identify its zeroes and determine their multiplicities, explaining how the graph and degree constrain them.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=12mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-2.4,0)--(2.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2.5)--(0,1.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-2:2.5,samples=160,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.15*(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x-2)});
\draw[black] (-1,0.06)--(-1,-0.06);
\draw[black] (2,0.06)--(2,-0.06);
\path (-1,0) node[above] {$-1$};
\path (2,0) node[below right] {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The sketch shows a monic quartic polynomial that touches the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=2$. Find its equation and its $y$-intercept.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=12mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-2,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-0.6)--(0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1.7:2.7,samples=160,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.3*(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x-2)*(\x-2)});
\draw[black] (-1,0.06)--(-1,-0.06);
\draw[black] (2,0.06)--(2,-0.06);
\path (-1,0) node[below] {$-1$};
\path (2,0) node[below] {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Each touch requires even multiplicity at least $2$. Together they account for degree $4$, so both zeroes are double.

The polynomial is monic, giving $P(x)=(x+1)^{2}(x-2)^{2}$.

$\begin{aligned}P(0)&=(1)^{2}(-2)^{2}\\&=4\end{aligned}$

The $y$-intercept is $(0,4)$.

Annotated sketch:

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=3.6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,<->] (-2,0)--(3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,<->] (0,-2)--(0,8.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->,domain=-1.7:2.7,samples=160,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{(\x+1)*(\x+1)*(\x-2)*(\x-2)});
\fill[black] (-1,0) circle (1.3pt);
\fill[black] (2,0) circle (1.3pt);
\fill[black] (0,4) circle (1.3pt);
\path (-1,0) node[below] {$-1$};
\path (2,0) node[below] {$2$};
\path (0,4) node[left] {$(0,4)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

